WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.192
The project asks students to build a small Spark SQL pipeline.

00:00:04.192 --> 00:00:07.477
First, inspect the source schema and grain.

00:00:07.477 --> 00:00:11.397
Second, clean and type the needed columns.

00:00:11.397 --> 00:00:15.352
Third, summarize monthly product revenue or readmission intervals.

00:00:15.352 --> 00:00:18.947
Finally, validate output grain and totals.

00:00:18.947 --> 00:00:25.602
In Spark, validation is still essential because distributed execution can make mistakes expensive.

00:00:25.602 --> 00:00:32.327
Connect this idea to a retail pipeline aggregating high-volume sales data across partitions.

00:00:32.327 --> 00:00:37.722
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:37.722 --> 00:00:45.247
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:45.247 --> 00:00:51.082
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:51.082 --> 00:00:56.960
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
